WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.353
A limit describes nearby outputs, even when the input itself is missing

00:00:05.353 --> 00:00:09.893
This quotient is undefined at one because its denominator is zero

00:00:09.893 --> 00:00:14.433
The graph can still approach a definite height from both sides.

00:00:15.733 --> 00:00:20.863
Factor the entire numerator as x minus one times x plus one

00:00:20.863 --> 00:00:27.702
For nearby inputs other than one, the shared factor is nonzero, so its ratio equals one

00:00:27.702 --> 00:00:30.267
Cancelling factors preserves these nearby values.

00:00:31.567 --> 00:00:35.193
The simpler expression approaches two as x approaches one

00:00:35.193 --> 00:00:40.834
Therefore the original quotient has limit two, although it has no value at one

00:00:40.834 --> 00:00:45.267
Taking a limit and assigning a function value are different operations.

00:00:46.567 --> 00:00:49.820
Both sides must agree for a two-sided limit

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:56.733
Absolute x divided by x equals negative one on the left and positive one on the right

00:00:56.733 --> 00:01:00.800
These different destination heights prevent a two-sided limit at zero.
